/**
@mainpage KMIPKit C11 Client Library Reference

@section intro_sec 1. Overview and Architecture
`KMIPKit` provides a stable C11 Application Binary Interface (ABI) for interacting with OASIS KMIP 2.1 servers.
All C functions adhere to strict memory ownership semantics, opaque pointers, panic-containment boundaries, and thread-local detailed error reporting.

---

@section install_sec 2. Installation and Linkage

@subsection pkgconfig Via pkg-config
```bash
pkg-config --cflags --libs kmipkit
```

@subsection cmake Via CMake
```cmake
find_package(kmipkit REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my_app PRIVATE kmipkit::kmipkit)
```

### Shared Libraries
- **Linux**: `libkmipkit.so.1`
- **macOS**: `libkmipkit.1.dylib`
- **Windows**: `kmipkit.dll` and `kmipkit.lib`

---

@section quickstart_sec 3. Quickstart Example

```c
#include <kmipkit/kmipkit.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    kmipkit_status_t status;
    kmipkit_client_t* client = NULL;

    // 1. Configure mTLS connection
    kmipkit_config_t config;
    memset(&config, 0, sizeof(config));
    config.struct_size = sizeof(config);
    config.abi_version = KMIPKIT_ABI_VERSION_1;
    config.endpoint = "kms.internal.corp:5696";
    config.ca_cert_path = "/etc/kmip/ca.pem";
    config.client_cert_path = "/etc/kmip/client.crt";
    config.client_key_path = "/etc/kmip/client.key";

    status = kmipkit_client_connect(&config, &client);
    if (status != KMIPKIT_STATUS_OK) {
        char err_buf[512];
        kmipkit_get_last_error(err_buf, sizeof(err_buf));
        fprintf(stderr, "Connection failed: %s\n", err_buf);
        return 1;
    }

    // 2. Create AES-256 Symmetric Key
    kmipkit_key_create_params_t params;
    memset(&params, 0, sizeof(params));
    params.struct_size = sizeof(params);
    params.algorithm = KMIPKIT_CRYPTO_ALGORITHM_AES;
    params.length_bits = 256;
    params.usage_mask = KMIPKIT_USAGE_ENCRYPT | KMIPKIT_USAGE_DECRYPT;

    char key_id[64];
    size_t key_id_len = sizeof(key_id);
    status = kmipkit_client_create_symmetric_key(client, &params, key_id, &key_id_len);
    if (status == KMIPKIT_STATUS_OK) {
        printf("Created Key ID: %s\n", key_id);
    }

    // 3. Cleanup and destroy client
    kmipkit_client_free(client);
    return 0;
}
```

---

@section transport_sec 4. Connection and Transport Configuration
The C ABI supports `KMIPKIT_TRANSPORT_RAW_TLS` (default, port 5696) and `KMIPKIT_TRANSPORT_HTTPS` (port 5697).
Mutual TLS 1.3 is enforced on all connections. Hostname validation cannot be disabled in production.

---

@section memory_sec 5. Memory Ownership and Secret Handles
1. **Input Buffers**: Callers retain ownership of input buffers (`const char*`, `const uint8_t*`). They are borrowed only for the duration of the API call.
2. **Output Strings**: Callers allocate destination buffers and provide length pointers (`char* buf, size_t* len`).
3. **Secret Handles**: Returned secrets use `kmipkit_secret_t*` opaque handles. Callers must invoke `kmipkit_secret_free()` to zeroize memory.

---

@section errors_sec 6. Error Handling
Every exported API function returns `kmipkit_status_t`. Detailed thread-local diagnostics are obtained with `kmipkit_get_last_error()`:

```c
kmipkit_status_t status = kmipkit_client_destroy(client, key_id);
if (status != KMIPKIT_STATUS_OK) {
    char err[512];
    kmipkit_get_last_error(err, sizeof(err));
    fprintf(stderr, "Destroy failed [Code %d]: %s\n", status, err);
}
```
*/
